      "id": 119654,
      "name": "livingintruefreedom",
      "username": "livingintruefreedom",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/l/ec9cab/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2014-11-12T07:33:42.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Ok, simplist answer I have. Lets use RA's Imbue Fire card for a perfect example of why this order of \"type change, amount modifiers, redirect\" is so important. RA's imbue has it listed in text order: +1 to Fire Damage, Change all hero damage to Fire, Power: Destroy this card. As you can see if you followed the cards text in order the damage bonus would only apply to damage that was already Fire type. Now using the order of \"type change, amount modifiers, redirect\" it now boosts that damage of all heros by 1, because their damage type is changed before the damage modifier is done.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Now here is there sucky part of Damage Type Change. Say your playing RA, and play Imbue Fire. But your in Megaoplis and <strong>after</strong> you played Imbue Fire, the environment flips Closed Quarter Combat. You have two different damage type changes. So you now follow the rule of Order of Play. Since CQC was played Last, it is the type of damage done, which means it is melee damage.</p>",
      "post_number": 6,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:52:55.451Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
